Levels (Crucial) The QNN SDK allows you to set runtime execution priorities (QNN_PRIORITY_LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH) when you instantiate a graph context. Set your Qwen3.5-0.8B File Manager context to QNN_PRIORITY_LOW. Set your Qwen3.5-4B / Gemma Executor context to QNN_PRIORITY_HIGH. Why this works: When your 4B tool caller needs to spit out words to the user, the Snapdragon hardware scheduler will instantly pause the 0.8B background wiki scanner mid-cycle, execute the user-facing token, and then resume the background memory scan seamlessly. 2\\. Gemma 4 Note: You Don't Need TFLite Good news on your Gemma double-check—you do not have to fall back to a heavy TFLite container to hit the HTP. Qualcomm built day-zero \"GenieX\" optimization (their custom high-performance llama.cpp port) right into the ecosystem \\[aihub.qualcomm.com\\]. Both the Gemma 4 E2B and E4B variants have native Qualcomm AI Hub community compilation support, allowing them to sit right alongside Qwen on the native QNN backend via your LLM_ACP pipeline without any conversion bottlenecks. 3\\. Chunk Your Token Prefills The biggest spike that will \"jam\" your pipeline is when the 0.8B model digests a large wiki text block all at once (the Prompt Prefill phase). In your backend execution loop, enforce a Max Prefill Chunk Size of 512 tokens for the 0.8B model. This breaks down long files into tiny, bite-sized compute chunks, leaving the HTP constantly open to intercept priority requests from your 4B master model. 📊 The Concurrent RAM Check Since you are loading them both into the HTP backend simultaneously via QAIRT: \\[Pre-Compiled Qwen3.5-0.8B\\] -\\> Consumes ~1.2 GB RAM (HTP Context) \\+ \\[GGUF/GenieX Qwen3.5-4B\\] -\\> Consumes ~3.8 GB RAM (HTP Context) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── Total Coexistent Footprint -\\> ~5.0 GB RAM Because 5.0 GB is strictly under your 6 GB standard open state, the Android OS will keep both contexts alive in memory. Your Mem0 pipeline can call the 0.8B manager to pull facts, pass the string buffer to your 4B tool executor, and run the whole agent entirely on the Hexagon NPU without a single CPU or GPU drop. Are you writing the orchestration code to pass the text strings between the two models in Python, or are you building the routing thread using C++ to talk directly to the QNN driver? So, I'm taking a ditch in the 0.08 and just going with the Quen 2B as my executive files management model. Either the Quen 2B at times or I can swap that out for the Gemma 4E2B. And so, my question is, while I'm running those, should I stick with the Gemma 4E4B while I'm while I have the smaller Gemma as the as the files executive or should I mix and match? Because on one hand, you got one the"}
{"source": "#AESOP_HORIZONS-UI_Master/(AESOP.]build/#QWEN_MODELS/On-device_Model-Guide/Model Query and half truth's from a slightly delusional Gemini .md", "chunk_index": 7, "heading": "**🎯 The Perfect Division of Labor**", "text": "that out for the Gemma 4E2B. And so, my question is, while I'm running those, should I stick with the Gemma 4E4B while I'm while I have the smaller Gemma as the as the files executive or should I mix and match? Because on one hand, you got one the Gemma E4B and the both the Gemmas and the small um and the E2 and the the 2B Quen all are compiled for the hexagon through a Qualcomm. But the the Quen the Quen 4B is not. So, I'm wondering if I should run both Quens at the same time or if I should mix and match and have a Quen and a small Gemma or uh the and or the Quen the small Quen and the big Quen at the same time. Ditching the 0.8B model for a 2B file executive model is a much better choice because sub-1B models struggle with complex file-path logic. To get the absolute smoothest performance on your HTPv79 (Snapdragon 8 Elite) within your 6 GB RAM budget, you should avoid mixing and matching architectures. Stick to an all-Qwen layout or an all-Gemma layout. ❌ Why Mixing Qwen and Gemma is a Bad Idea Mixing a Qwen model with a Gemma model forces your system to load two completely separate tokenizers and runtime memory pools into your RAM. Tokenizer Overhead: Qwen uses a 151,643 vocabulary size, while Gemma uses a massive 256,000 vocabulary size. Keeping both token arrays active in RAM simultaneously wastes precious megabytes before inference even starts. \\[1\\] Context Fragmentation: The GGML / LLM_ACP translation layer handles memory caching differently for dense architectures like Gemma than for linear ones like Qwen. Forcing the HTP driver to constantly jump back and forth between two fundamentally different graph layouts will create micro-stutters. 🏆 Option 1: The All-Qwen Setup (Best for Vibe Coding & Speed) Configuration: Qwen3.5-2B (AI Hub Native Binary) + Qwen3.5-4B (Your Custom Q4_0 GGUF Pipeline). How it handles the HTP: This is the most efficient configuration. Because both models share the exact same structural logic and early-fusion token vocabulary, the HTPv79 driver can reuse its internal attention matrix cache. The RAM Footprint: Qwen3.5-2B (Native): ~2.2 GB Qwen3.5-4B (Q4_0): ~3.6 GB Total: ~5.8 GB. It rides right on your 6 GB limit, but because Qwen uses flat linear attention, your RAM will never spike beyond this ceiling, keeping your system safe from crashes. The Performance: You get blazing-fast file parsing from the native 2B model, and completely flat, un-throttled throughput when vibe coding with the 4B model. 🥈 Option 2: The All-Gemma Setup (Best for Maximum Accuracy) Configuration: Gemma 4 E2B (AI Hub Native) + Gemma 4 E4B (AI Hub Native or Q4_0 GGUF). How it handles the HTP: Since both models are fully compiled and optimized via the Qualcomm ecosystem, they bypass translation friction entirely. They will communicate with the HTP driver seamlessly. The RAM Footprint: Gemma 4 E2B (Native): ~2.2 GB Gemma 4 E4B (Native): ~3.5 GB Total: ~5.7 GB. The"}
